Contains checks whether a value belongs to the set. Count gives the number of distinct members, not the number of attempted additions.

Contains(value) checks membership without adding anything and returns a Boolean. In this separate example:
```csharp
var sample = new System.Collections.Generic.HashSet<int> { 4 };
System.Console.WriteLine(sample.Contains(4));
System.Console.WriteLine(sample.Contains(7));
```
Output: True, then False.

File.ReadAllText returns the file contents as a string. A successful write followed by a read can verify the value you stored; printing a path alone cannot.

This separate temporary-file example appends B after A. AppendAllText(path, text) returns no value; a read-back observes the stored result. The finally block attempts cleanup even if I/O fails:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
string samplePath = Path.GetTempFileName();
try {
    File.WriteAllText(samplePath, "A");
    File.AppendAllText(samplePath, "B");
    Console.WriteLine(File.ReadAllText(samplePath));
}
finally { File.Delete(samplePath); }
```
Output: AB.

If Equals says two values are equal, GetHashCode must return the same code for them in that execution. Different values may still share a hash; a hash collision is not proof of equality.

Equals(other) tests logical equality and returns a Boolean. GetHashCode() takes no arguments and returns a hash code; compare equal values’ hashes rather than expecting a fixed number:
```csharp
using System;
var a = new SampleKey("Read");
var b = new SampleKey("Read");
Console.WriteLine(a.Equals(b));
Console.WriteLine(a.Equals(new SampleKey("Write")));
Console.WriteLine(a.GetHashCode() == b.GetHashCode());
public record SampleKey(string Name);
```
Output: True, then False, then True.

ToArray makes a separate array. Changing that copy does not change the source; choose a view or a copy deliberately.

ToArray() takes no arguments and returns a new array containing the view’s elements. This separate example changes only that copy:
```csharp
using System;
int[] original = { 4, 5 };
int[] copy = original.AsSpan(0, 1).ToArray();
copy[0] = 9;
Console.WriteLine($"{original[0]} {copy[0]}");
```
Output: 4 9.

size() can inspect a const string. Calling a mutating operation such as clear() through this const reference is rejected.

On a mutable string, clear() takes no arguments, removes all characters and returns no value. This is separate from the const parameter below:
```cpp
std::string text{"Moon"};
text.clear();
std::cout << text.size() << "\n";
```
Output: 0.

operator[] is different: scores["Zed"] inserts the missing key with a value-initialized int, which is 0. Use find or contains for observation; use [] when insertion is intended.

contains(key) returns a Boolean without inserting. In this separate example, indexing the absent key then inserts the default int value zero:
```cpp
std::map<std::string,int> sample{{"Ada",2}};
std::cout << std::boolalpha << sample.contains("Zed") << "\n";
std::cout << sample["Zed"] << " " << sample.size() << "\n";
```
Output: false, then 0 2.

value_or(defaultValue) can supply a fallback without altering the optional. It is useful only when treating absence as that fallback fits the application.

value_or(9) returns the contained int or the supplied fallback. has_value() takes no arguments and reports whether a value is present; it confirms the fallback did not fill the empty optional:
```cpp
std::optional<int> present{0};
std::optional<int> absent;
std::cout << present.value_or(9) << " " << absent.value_or(9) << "\n";
std::cout << std::boolalpha << absent.has_value() << "\n";
```
Output: 0 9, then false.

Methods such as toUpperCase return a result rather than rewriting the original String object. Assign a returned result if you want a variable to refer to it.

toUpperCase() takes no arguments and returns uppercased text using the default locale. These letters give this result; locale-sensitive text can differ between locales. The original String is unchanged:
```java
String original = "moon";
String upper = original.toUpperCase();
System.out.println(original + " " + upper);
```
Output: moon MOON.

If equals returns true, the two objects must have the same hashCode. Unequal objects may still collide; a hash code is not a unique identifier. Records generate compatible equals and hashCode implementations.

hashCode() takes no arguments and returns an int hash. After the Key record declaration shown below, this checks the equal-value requirement without assuming any particular hash number:
```java
Key first = new Key(3);
Key second = new Key(3);
System.out.println(first.hashCode() == second.hashCode());
```
Output: true.

== on two wrapper references tests identity, and caching can make results misleading. Use equals for non-null logical wrapper equality or compare deliberately unboxed primitives when safe.

For a non-null Integer receiver, equals(other) returns whether the other object is an Integer with the same int value. It does not ask whether wrapper references share an object:
```java
Integer left = 400;
Integer same = 400;
Integer other = 401;
System.out.println(left.equals(same));
System.out.println(left.equals(other));
```
Output: true, then false.

ArrayList is mutable, so sort can reorder it. List.of is unmodifiable and cannot itself be sorted in place. reversed applied to the complete comparator reverses both primary and tie-break ordering.

reversed() takes no arguments and returns the reverse comparator; it does not sort a list until sort uses it. In this separate example:
```java
var sample = new java.util.ArrayList<>(java.util.List.of("aa", "z", "bb"));
java.util.Comparator<String> order = java.util.Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)
    .thenComparing(java.util.Comparator.naturalOrder());
sample.sort(order.reversed());
System.out.println(sample);
```
Output: [bb, aa, z].

Collections.unmodifiableList wraps a live view of the original list. It prevents edits through that view but still reflects edits through the original reference. Neither operation makes arbitrary element objects deeply immutable.

unmodifiableList(source) returns a view that blocks edits through that reference, not through source. In this separate example:
```java
var source = new java.util.ArrayList<>(java.util.List.of("Read"));
var view = java.util.Collections.unmodifiableList(source);
source.add("Write");
System.out.println(view);
```
Output: [Read, Write].

